\subsection{Performance Analysis}
\label{subsec:performance-analysis}

This subsection presents the experimental design used to evaluate the runtime behavior of PLOSHA-RMFR under dynamic IIoT edge--fog conditions. The objective is to assess whether the proposed framework can improve aggregation efficiency, reduce recovery overhead, and maintain service availability under workload fluctuation, incomplete aggregation, and fog-node failures.

\subsubsection{Experimental Setup}

The evaluation considers an edge--fog IIoT environment consisting of multiple sensors, fog nodes, and a cloud server. Sensors continuously generate time-series industrial measurements and transmit encrypted reports to their assigned fog nodes. Each fog node performs adaptive micro-slot aggregation and invokes RMFR when overload, incomplete aggregation, or failure is detected.

The experiments compare PLOSHA-RMFR with four representative schemes: Ref.~\cite{ref22}, Ref.~\cite{ref24}, Ref.~\cite{ref37}, and Ref.~\cite{ref38}. Ref.~\cite{ref22} represents learning-based adaptive scheduling, Ref.~\cite{ref24} represents privacy-preserving aggregation, Ref.~\cite{ref37} represents hybrid fault-tolerant workflow scheduling, and Ref.~\cite{ref38} represents fault-tolerant serverless edge stream processing.

Unless otherwise stated, the number of sensors is varied from 500 to 5000, the number of fog nodes is varied from 5 to 50, and each aggregation epoch is divided into adaptive micro-slots. The failure rate is varied from 2\% to 20\%, and the workload intensity is varied by changing the sensor reporting rate. Each experiment is repeated multiple times, and the average value is reported.


\subsubsection*{\textbf{Experiment 1: Ablation of the PLOSHA Aggregation Architecture}}

This experiment evaluates the individual and joint contributions of adaptive micro-slot partitioning and hierarchical aggregation, independently of the TEE and cryptographic implementation.
The complete PLOSHA architecture is compared with the following variants:

\begin{enumerate}

\item \textbf{Flat-Epoch}: all encrypted sensor readings
received during an aggregation epoch are processed as a single
aggregation unit without micro-slot partitioning.

\item \textbf{Fixed-Slot}: each aggregation epoch is divided
into a fixed number of micro-slots regardless of the predicted
processing capacity, reliability, or failure exposure.

\item \textbf{Adaptive-Slot}: the number of micro-slots is
adaptively selected according to the predicted operating
conditions, but completed micro-slot aggregates are not
maintained as reusable hierarchical states. Consequently,
aggregation recovery requires recomputation of the affected
aggregation results rather than reuse of previously completed
micro-slot aggregates.

\item \textbf{Full PLOSHA}: both prediction-driven adaptive
micro-slot partitioning and reusable hierarchical aggregation
are enabled.

\end{enumerate}

All variants employ the same TEE-assisted ciphertext transformation, Paillier cryptographic parameters, aggregation epoch duration, fog hardware, and sensor data. The number of sensors is varied from 500 to 5000 in increments of 500. The number of fog nodes scales proportionally to maintain a constant sensor-to-fog ratio of 100 sensors per fog node across the sweep.

A failure rate of 10\% is applied to stress-test recovery behavior, as lower failure rates produce insufficient recovery events to meaningfully differentiate the ablation variants. To evaluate fault resilience under different failure timings, a fog-node interruption is injected after 25\%, 50\%, and 75\% of each aggregation epoch, representing early-, mid-, and late-stage aggregation failures, respectively. Each configuration is repeated 30 times, with ten repetitions for each failure timing, using identical workload and failure traces to ensure a fair comparison. The mean values are reported.

The evaluation metrics include end-to-end aggregation latency, aggregation CPU time, aggregation-loss exposure, recomputation overhead, and the number of reused completed
micro-slot aggregates. The results are shown in
Fig.~\ref{fig:exp1_ablation}.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{exp1_ablation_aggregation.png}
\caption{Ablation results of the PLOSHA aggregation
architecture under varying workload and failure conditions.}
\label{fig:exp1_ablation}
\end{figure}

As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:exp1_ablation}, aggregation latency increases with the number of sensors for all variants. Under stable conditions, Flat-Epoch introduces minimal slot-management overhead. However, it exhibits the highest aggregation-loss exposure and recomputation overhead when a failure occurs because the entire aggregation epoch must be reconstructed.

Fixed-Slot confines recovery to smaller aggregation regions. However, its static granularity cannot adapt to changing workload and reliability conditions. Consequently, it may introduce unnecessary slot-management overhead under stable conditions or provide insufficient fault isolation under adverse conditions.

Adaptive-Slot dynamically adjusts the aggregation granularity according to predicted processing capacity, reliability, and failure exposure. This adaptation provides a better balance between aggregation overhead and fault isolation. However, because completed micro-slot aggregates are not preserved, recovery still requires recomputation of the affected aggregation results.

Full PLOSHA combines adaptive partitioning with hierarchical aggregation, allowing previously completed micro-slot aggregates to be reused during recovery while only the affected aggregation regions are recomputed. Consequently, Full PLOSHA achieves the lowest interruption-induced aggregation latency and recomputation overhead while maintaining low aggregation-loss exposure across the evaluated workload conditions.

These results demonstrate that adaptive partitioning determines an appropriate aggregation granularity according to predicted operating conditions, whereas hierarchical aggregation enables intermediate aggregate reuse and localized recomputation. Their combination therefore provides the best balance between aggregation efficiency and fault resilience.




\subsubsection*{\textbf{Experiment 2: Scheduling Efficiency and Workload Imbalance}}

This experiment evaluates scheduling efficiency under increasing
fog-system scale. PLOSHA-RMFR is compared with Ref~\cite{ref22},
Ref~\cite{ref37}, and Ref~\cite{ref38}. The number of fog nodes is
varied from 5 to 50, with each node initialized under heterogeneous
processing capacity, queue occupancy, and communication latency
profiles. All schemes receive identical workload traces and node-state
information.

Scheduling latency is measured from receipt of a workload request and candidate-node states until a fog node is selected. Offline training, state collection, workload transmission, and execution are excluded.
Each configuration is repeated 30 times, and the average scheduling latency with standard deviation is reported. The results are plotted on a logarithmic scale to accommodate the multi-order-of-magnitude differences across schemes and are shown in
Fig.~\ref{fig:scheduling_efficiency}.

\begin{figure}[t]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{exp2_scheduling_efficiency_line.png}
    \caption{Scheduling latency under increasing numbers of heterogeneous fog nodes (log scale).}
    \label{fig:scheduling_efficiency}
\end{figure}

As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:scheduling_efficiency}, scheduling latency increases with the number of candidate fog nodes for all schemes except FedDQN, which maintains a near-constant latency of approximately 3.5~$\mu$s due to its pre-trained DQN policy producing fixed-cost inference regardless of the scheduling space. However, FedDQN's reliance on offline training limits its adaptability to runtime workload changes.

Ref~\cite{ref38} exhibits the worst scaling behavior, with latency increasing by nearly an order of magnitude from approximately 37~$\mu$s at 5 nodes to over 330~$\mu$s at 50 nodes, reflecting the overhead of its serverless function orchestration and container placement model. Ref~\cite{ref37} demonstrates moderate growth, scaling from approximately 0.25~$\mu$s to 1.3~$\mu$s as the number of candidate nodes increases.

In contrast, PLOSHA-RMFR achieves the lowest scheduling latency across all fog-node configurations, ranging from approximately 0.16~$\mu$s at 5 nodes to 0.72~$\mu$s at 50 nodes. This efficiency results from its lightweight EWMA-based prediction and single-pass candidate evaluation using predicted capacity, risk, and reliability scores, which avoids the online inference overhead of learning-based schemes and the iterative placement search of orchestration-based approaches. These results demonstrate that PLOSHA-RMFR provides responsive and scalable scheduling under heterogeneous IIoT fog conditions.

\subsubsection*{\textbf{Experiment 3: Impact of Failure Rate}}

This experiment evaluates the fault resilience of PLOSHA-RMFR under increasing fog-node failures. The failure rate is varied from 2\% to 20\% by randomly introducing node outages during aggregation epochs. PLOSHA-RMFR is compared with Refs.~\cite{ref22}, \cite{ref37}, and \cite{ref38}. The primary evaluation metric is recovery latency, while aggregation completeness and system availability are used as secondary metrics.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{exp3_failure_rate.png}
\caption{Recovery latency versus fog-node failure rate.}
\label{exp3_failure_rate}
\end{figure}

As shown in Fig.~\ref{exp3_failure_rate}, PLOSHA-RMFR
consistently achieves the lowest recovery latency across all
failure rates. Ref.~\cite{ref37} also maintains low latency,
indicating that its workflow recovery mechanism responds
efficiently under the evaluated fault conditions. However,
PLOSHA-RMFR remains faster because predictive delegation
and localized micro-slot recovery limit restoration to affected
aggregation regions. FedDQN~\cite{ref22} exhibits higher
latency due to learning-based recovery decisions, while
Ref.~\cite{ref38} incurs the largest delay from service
migration and placement reconfiguration. Overall,
PLOSHA-RMFR provides the lowest recovery latency while
additionally supporting aggregation-loss localization and
encrypted aggregation continuity.



\subsubsection*{\textbf{Experiment 4: Aggregation-Loss Exposure}}

This experiment evaluates the ability of PLOSHA-RMFR to reduce aggregation-loss exposure under failure conditions. The objective is to quantify the fraction of aggregation results affected when failures occur during an aggregation epoch. The number of adaptive micro-slots is varied from 1 to 20 while maintaining a fixed workload and failure rate. PLOSHA-RMFR is compared with Ref.~\cite{ref24}, Ref.~\cite{ref37}, and Ref.~\cite{ref38}. The evaluation metric is aggregation-loss exposure, defined as the proportion of aggregation data that becomes unavailable or requires recomputation following a failure. 

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{exp4_loss_exposure.png}
\caption{Aggregation-loss exposure versus number of micro-slots.}
\label{fig:exp4}
\end{figure}

As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:exp4}, aggregation-loss exposure
decreases as the number of micro-slots increases for
PLOSHA-RMFR, whereas Refs.~\cite{ref24} and~\cite{ref37}
remain nearly constant because their recovery scopes are not
adjusted according to aggregation granularity. PLOSHA-RMFR
confines a failure to the affected micro-slot while preserving
completed intermediate aggregates, thereby preventing
unaffected aggregation results from being recomputed.
Accordingly, its maximum loss exposure follows the
$1/m^{*}$ bound and decreases rapidly as finer partitioning is
applied. Consequently, only a small fraction of the aggregation
epoch requires recovery, reducing failure-induced data loss
and recovery overhead. These results confirm that adaptive
hierarchical aggregation effectively localizes failures while
improving the resilience of encrypted aggregation.

\subsubsection*{\textbf{Experiment 5: Recovery Communication Overhead}}

This experiment evaluates the communication overhead incurred during fault recovery. The number of incomplete micro-slots is varied while maintaining a fixed aggregation workload and failure rate. PLOSHA-RMFR is compared with Refs.~\cite{ref37} and \cite{ref38}, which perform recovery through workflow resubmission, task replication, service migration, or standby reconfiguration. The evaluation metric is the amount of recovery-related data exchanged between fog nodes, measured in kilobytes (KB). 

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{exp5_recovery_comm.png}
\caption{Recovery communication overhead versus number of incomplete micro-slots.}
\label{fig:exp5}
\end{figure}

As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:exp5}, recovery communication
overhead increases with the number of incomplete
micro-slots for PLOSHA-RMFR and Ref.~\cite{ref38},
whereas Ref.~\cite{ref37} exhibits only moderate variation.
PLOSHA-RMFR consistently incurs the lowest communication
overhead because RMFR transfers only the ciphertexts and
recovery metadata associated with affected micro-slots. In
contrast, Ref.~\cite{ref37} requires workflow-state transfer
during task resubmission, resulting in relatively stable
communication overhead once the recovery workflow is
established. Ref.~\cite{ref38} incurs the highest overhead
because service migration requires transferring larger
execution states and placement information as the recovery
scope expands. Consequently, PLOSHA-RMFR confines
communication to the affected aggregation regions,
significantly improving recovery communication efficiency.




\subsubsection*{\textbf{Experiment 6: Effectiveness of AFLTO}}

This experiment evaluates the contribution of the Adaptive Feedback Learning and Threshold Optimization (AFLTO) mechanism. We compared the complete PLOSHA-RMFR framework with a variant in which AFLTO is disabled and all aggregation and recovery thresholds remain static throughout system operation. The workload intensity and fog-node failure rate are dynamically varied over time to emulate realistic IIoT environments with changing operational conditions. The evaluation metrics include aggregation completeness, recovery frequency, and system availability. 

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{exp6_aflto.png}
\caption{Impact of AFLTO on aggregation completeness and system availability.}
\label{fig:exp6}
\end{figure}

As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:exp6}, enabling AFLTO improves
aggregation completeness from $0.877$ to $0.920$ and system
availability from $0.974$ to $1.000$. With static thresholds,
the framework cannot respond effectively to changing workload
and failure conditions, resulting in delayed or unnecessary
recovery actions. In contrast, AFLTO continuously adjusts the
aggregation and recovery thresholds according to observed
completeness, reliability, and recovery urgency. Consequently,
PLOSHA-RMFR responds earlier to adverse conditions while
avoiding excessive recovery, thereby maintaining more complete
aggregation and higher service availability.

